417] [17592186409582 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409582 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409582 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409568 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409582 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "bc790e07-5a28-514e-aa55-ed9af585fbba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409569 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409570 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409572 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186409573 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186409566 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409583 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "eea492f1-156d-5834-96c0-8454fc274282" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409584 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409581 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409584 #edb/ref 1069 "q-182506" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409584 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409584 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- $A⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡A∧B$\n- $¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬A∧B$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409584 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1308109d-5efe-51ad-bfa1-4050522344dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409584 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine each of our statements:\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, $A⇒¬B$ is logically equivalent to the disjunction $¬A∨¬B,$ which by De Morgan's law is equivalent to $¬(A∧B).$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. From part I, we have the following equivalence:\n\n$$\nA⇒¬B≡¬(A∧B)\n$$\n\nBy negating this statement, we get\n\n$$\n¬(A⇒¬B)≡¬(¬(A∧B)).\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negation, we reach\n\n$$\nA∧B.\n$$\n\nSo, $¬(A⇒¬B)$ is equivalent to $A∧B.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409586 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186409583 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409586 #edb/ref 1069 "q-81892" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409586 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409586 #edb/ref 1071 "Simplify the statement $(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409586 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c7953127-b916-5d8d-98b4-eeece8a2e1ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409586 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n(¬B∧C)⇒(B∨¬C)≡¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nWe can distribute the negation over the disjunction using De Morgan's law:\n\n$$\n¬(¬B∧C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)\n$$\n\nCanceling out double negations, we reach\n\n$$\n(¬(¬B)∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C).\n$$\n\nFinally, we simplify the disjunction of a statement with itself:\n\n$$\n(B∨¬C)∨(B∨¬C)≡B∨¬C\n$$\n\nSo the given statement is equivalent to $B∨¬C.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409587 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e76a699-413c-5c79-9a86-67728b6bb289" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409587 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409584 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409588 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409589 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409588 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b1214e01-49e9-573c-a4c8-4d572ee5620a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409588 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409586 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409589 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186409587 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409589 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "ede73126-39fd-544c-9a47-ac55caf10274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186409589 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186409585 #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
